I previously installed Ubuntu LTS 24.x on R2D2, and the install works, but when you boot, you get a splash screen, then it goes black.
I set this aside for months, but I'm back at it.

!!Step 1 - Get It To Boot So You Can See Desktop

The following instructions worked for me.

Prompt> I have installed Ubuntu LTS 24, and the mac boots up, I see a splash screen, then the screen goes black.  I rebooted and am in grub.  How do I fix this black screen stuff?

This black screen happens because Ubuntu 24.04 tries to use the default radeon kernel driver, which crashes when it encounters the unique dual AMD FirePro (D300/D500/D700) setup inside the 2013 Mac Pro. 

Since you are already sitting at the GRUB menu, you can fix this permanently by forcing Ubuntu to use the newer amdgpu driver framework. 

Step 1: Temporarily Boot into Ubuntu

First, we need to bypass the broken graphics driver so you can reach your desktop: 

# In the GRUB menu, make sure the top Ubuntu option is highlighted and press the e key on your keyboard to edit the boot parameters.
# Use your arrow keys to scroll down to the bottom paragraph. Look for the long line that starts with linux (it usually ends with something like ro quiet splash $vt_handoff).
# Move your cursor to the very end of that line, add a space, and type: 

```
nomodeset
```

# Press F10 (or Ctrl + X) to boot. 

Your Mac Pro will now boot up using basic, low-resolution "safe" graphics. It might look pixelated or slow, but you will reach your desktop. 

Step 2: Make the Fix Permanent

Now that you are on your Ubuntu desktop, we need to permanently tell GRUB to map the FirePro cards correctly: 

# Press Ctrl + Alt + T to open a Terminal window.
# Open the GRUB configurations file using a text editor by running:

```
sudo nano /etc/default/grub
```

# Type your password when prompted (no characters will show on screen while typing, just hit Enter).
# Look for the line that reads:

```
GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="quiet splash"
```

# Change that line to exactly this to disable the old driver and force the modern AMD stack:

```
GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT="quiet splash radeon.si_support=0 amdgpu.si_support=1 amdgpu.dc=1"
```

# Save the file by pressing Ctrl + O, then hit Enter. Exit the editor by pressing Ctrl + X.

Step 3: Update and Reboot

For Ubuntu to recognize the changes you just saved, you have to refresh the boot manager:

# Run the update command in your terminal:

```
sudo update-grub
```

2. Once it finishes processing, restart your Mac Pro: 

```
sudo reboot
```

Upon restarting, your Mac Pro should cruise right past the splash screen and boot directly into a perfectly scaled, hardware-accelerated desktop environment.

Once you are successfully logged in, let me know if you need help getting the Wi-Fi/Bluetooth working or configuring fan controls so your Mac Pro doesn't overheat under Linux load!

!! Installing Fan Control on Mac Pro 2013

```
apt install macfanctld
vi /etc/macfanctl.conf
# set these two as follows:
# temp_avg_floor: 45
# temp_avg_ceiling: 80

sudo systemctl restart macfanctld
sudo systemctl enable macfanctld

sudo apt install lm-sensors -y
sensors   # show temps of important stuff
```

!! Wifi & Bluetooth on Mac Pro 2013
While The Google warns that these won't work (on early Ubuntu 24 LTS), it appears the fixes are in, so using CAT5 cable I was able to get it working:

```
sudo apt update && sudo apt install broadcom-sta-dkms -y
```

I then went to wifi settings, found my network and authenticated.  
All Seems Well

!!UBSAN Lockup

After an hour or so of use, the machine locked up tight.
Display was good, but nothing responded.
After reboot, look at the logs:

```
journalctl -b -1 -p err..emerg
```

The last few lines implicated something called UBSAN, which is ?????

```
kernel: UBSAN: array-index-out-of-bounds in src/wl/sys/wl_linux.c:1939:4
kernel: index 2 is out of range for type 'ether_addr'
```

This is the fix.
NB my wpa_supplicant.conf was not there, so I created it.

```
sudo vi /etc/wpa_supplicant/wpa_supplicant.conf

# add the following lines:
ctrl_interface=/var/run/wpa_supplicant
update_config=1
bgscan=""
```

Save the file and restart the daemon:

```
sudo systemctl restart NetworkManager
```

!!Remote Desktop

In system settings on R2D2 you can enable either cloning main desktop or remote login.
If you clone the main desktop and that session gets locked, you're hosed.
I opted for remote login.
Make sure to enable a hole in filewall for 3389/tcp.

You can use Microsoft RDP client (app store) for either of these.
For remote desktop, just append ":3389" to name of the machine.

There's a bug in RDF client.
Export the card containing connection details for your 3389 and edit it.
You will find a line that looks like this, ending in ZERO. 
Change the ZERO to ONE and import it back into RDF app, or just double click on the file.

```
use redirection server name:i:1
```

